> You can create a state variable to keep track of whether the user has made changes to the form data or not, and then pass that state variable as a prop to the SubmitButtons component.
> 
> Here's an example of how you can modify the code to achieve this:
> 
> ```tsx
> const [oldChanges, newChanges] = useMemo(
>   () => DiffPerformer(PerformerSchema.cast(fieldData), performer),
>   [fieldData, performer]
> );
> 
> const [hasChanges, setHasChanges] = useState(false);
> 
> useEffect(() => {
>   // Check if there are any changes
>   if (oldChanges.length > 0 || newChanges.length > 0) {
>     setHasChanges(true);
>   } else {
>     setHasChanges(false);
>   }
> }, [oldChanges, newChanges]);
> 
> return (
>   <>
>     <SubmitButtons hasChanges={hasChanges} />
>   </>
> );
> ```
> 
> In this code, we're creating a state variable called hasChanges using the useState hook. We're also using the useEffect hook to check if there are any changes in the form data, and updating the hasChanges state variable accordingly. Finally, we're passing the hasChanges prop to the SubmitButtons component.
> 
> Now, in the SubmitButtons component, you can use the hasChanges prop to conditionally show a confirmation dialog on cancel. For example:
> 
> ```tsx
> function SubmitButtons({ hasChanges }) {
>   const handleCancel = () => {
>     if (hasChanges) {
>       // Show confirmation dialog
>     } else {
>       // Navigate away without confirmation
>     }
>   };
> 
>   return (
>     <>
>       <button onClick={handleCancel}>Cancel</button>
>       <button type="submit">Submit</button>
>     </>
>   );
> }
> ```
> 
> In this example, we're checking the hasChanges prop inside the handleCancel function to determine whether to show a confirmation dialog or not. If hasChanges is true, we show the confirmation dialog, and if it's false, we navigate away without showing the dialog.
